A||N||6||Monkeyman||Amp rack||||1||
Z||000000||Monkeyman||05-30-2000||05:00 PM||||I need some help building an amp rack.  For various reasons, I'm planning on building a rack for my amp and CD changer in the rear trunk.  Here's my plan.  If you look in the trunk, you'll see a small ledge on either side.  I plan on building a board that will fit across those ledges.  I want to sink my amp in one side and my CD changer (vertically) in the other side.  <BR>A couple of problems with this.  1st, I'd have to build the board in 2 pieces or I won't be able to get it in place.  If I do this (I plan on covering it with original style carpet), how will I be able to get it back out if I ever need to?  I could cover the 2 boards separately, but I don't think it would look right.  I don't really see any reason why I'd need to remove it other than maintainance or to change wiring/components?  Maybe I could do that through the opening for the CD changer?<BR>Second problem,  I'm not much of a carpenter.  The local stereo shop wants $200 to build one and I can't part with that kind of $$.  Anyone good with wood?  Wanna make about $50??  (This is a serious question.)<BR>Last problem (for now).  Does anyone know of a source for the original style trunk carpet?<P>Like my friend FD says, "Thanks for the space."||||reg||1
Z||000001||olympic||05-30-2000||05:55 PM||||   Any local stereo shop should carry several shades of trunk carpeting, just pick one that matches.  It shouldn't cost more than $15/yard(3ftX3ft) which is all you should need. This carpet is great for beginners becaust mistakes are easily hidden and it's easy to apply and stretch.  Just cut it too fit, apply some carpet glue to the wood and stick the carpet on. Use staples to hold the carpet in place until the glue dries.  If you do a good lob you can't even see the seams because the "fuzz" on the carpet will hide them.  As for wood, use medium density fiberboard(MDF).  Its much easier to work with than plywood.  Just remember to predrill any screw holes because it will crack easily.<BR>   Here's some pics of my daily driver and it's show winning stereo system.  It pumps out 1600 watts and 140db and I installed it completely myself.  This was my first serious install.  I had installed a system in a previous car but it was not done for show, everything was just bolted in place wherever it would fit.  All it takes is lots of planning and patience.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/topaz04_1.jpg"> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/topaz05.jpg"> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/topaz06.jpg"> <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||BroCar 87||05-30-2000||11:37 PM||||Olympic, How do you like the ECLIPSE woofers? I am getting an entire ECLIPSE stereo set-up. Headunit, speakers, 8' and 10' sub, Amps, and CD changer. ALL ECLPISE. Just wonderin how you like em. I just recently heard about them. Is there a website that has all the info on these products? I haven't been able to find any info at all about Eclipse. Thanks ||||reg||1
Z||000003||olympic||05-31-2000||01:16 AM||||Hi Brocar 87,<BR>   I've had these subs for over a year now and they are awesome.  When I first saw them in magazines I knew I had to have them.  I think I had the first set in my province because I had them shipped directly from the factory.  There were no dealers here yet.  Anyways, each sub weighs about 30 lbs and requires 0.5-1.0 cubic feet of airspace(very small).  I built my enclosure to 1.0 cf per sub as recommended for a sound quality installation.  The smaller 0.5 cf number is for an SPL install. They are not recommended for a ported enclosure.<BR>    The sound quality is perfect.  These subs blur my vision at 80hz and can shake the ground all the way down to 10hz.  The peak to peak excursion is at least 2.5".<BR>   They are also stunning to look at.  It would be a shame to hide the magnet and basket assembly in a box without a window.<BR>   You'll need lots of power for them because they are not very efficient.  The model I have are the single 6 ohm voice coil version.  I wired them in parallel(3 ohms) and ran them in mono from my Alpine 1505 V12 Expert amp(1000 watts).  This seems to be about right for these subs, but I wouldn't hesitate to give them double that amount.  I would't try to run them with less than 300 watts each.  <BR>  BUY THESE SUBS, you'll love them.  And let me know which Eclipse head unit you get and how you like it.  My current deck is an older Alpine CD unit and is starting to act up.  I'm debating wheather to replace it with another Alpine or an Eclipse.<BR>  Eclipses' website is <A HREF="http://www.eclipse-web.com" TARGET=_blank>www.eclipse-web.com</A>    I just checked their site and they have added a couple new models, like the Titanium series.  Pretty cool stuff.  I also noticed that all their subs are either single or dual 4 ohm voice coils.  The single 6 ohm voice coil model(like mine) are gone.  Anyways, good luck and keep us posted!!!||||reg||1
Z||000004||Oreif||05-31-2000||02:20 AM||||If your putting the amp and CD changer into the rear trunk, I would invest in some braided shielding and don't bring the wires into the interior via the ECM/harness connector. Otherwise you will pick up so much noise you won't hear the stereo. The front trunk or behind the seats are easier and less interference from the engine noise.||||reg||1
Z||000005||80's BOY||05-31-2000||02:22 PM||||hey monkeyman, i had a similar idea for my trunk also. i was brainstorming about a "hinged" cover that outlined the amps and such. they would be mounted on a solid floor built halfway in the trunk with the cover fitting around the cutouts for the amps. as far as the problem with making it fit, i think that thick foam padding covering the corners would simulate the total piece of wood going all the way across, but would still let you take the piece out. you could cover this padding with carpet or vinyl and never tell!! just an idea....i'll shutup now.||||reg||3
Z||000006||Monkeyman||05-31-2000||04:27 PM||||Oreif--I had planned on installing my amp behind one of the seats (slightly sunken into the false firewall), but couldn't really come up with a good place to mount my CD changer (it's a fairly large chassis).  I have no intention of ever competeing (sp?), but I <B>do</B> want it to look good.  I can't really find a good way to mount them in the front trunk although I'd love to.  Seems like there's just too much stuff in the front (w/w stuff, A/C stuff, etc).  If you can make some suggestions as to where I can mount the amp and CD changer either in the front trunk or interior, please let me know.  I had originally planned to move the battery up front, too, but...  I'd like some kind of covered, custom mount.  I don't just want to screw everything to the front trunk liner.||||reg||1
